## Why This Is Asked

This question tests the ability to design a constrained dynamic programming solution for block partitioning problems with specific boundary conditions.

## Key Concepts

- Block partitioning with exact count and minimum length constraints
- Prefix sums for O(1) block value computation
- 2D DP with reconstruction pointers
- Time/space trade-offs and optimization opportunities

## Code Example

```javascript
// DP recurrence for blocks with minimum length L
for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
  for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
    dp[i][t] = -INF;
    for (let s = L; s <= i; s++) {
      dp[i][t] = Math.max(dp[i][t], dp[i-s][t-1] + (prefix[i] - prefix[i-s]));
    }
  }
}
```

## Implementation Considerations

- Time complexity: O(n × k × (n-L)) = O(n²k)
- Space: O(n × k) for DP table plus O(n) for prefix sums
- Add parent pointer array for reconstruction
- Base cases: dp[i][0] = 0, dp[0][t>0] = -∞

## Why This Is Asked

Explores a novel variation of LPS with deletions, forcing a 3D DP and careful reconstruction.

## Key Concepts

- 3D DP: i, j bounds plus remaining deletions t.
- Transitions handle match, deletion of either end, and base cases.
- Reconstruction by tracing choices from dp[0][n-1][k].
- Space optimization via rolling t or two 2D layers.

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxLPSWithDeletions(s, k) {
  const n = s.length;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(k+1).fill(0)));
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) dp[i][i][0] = 1;
  for (let len = 2; len <= n; len++) {
    for (let i = 0; i + len - 1 < n; i++) {
      const j = i + len - 1;
      for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
        if (s[i] === s[j]) {
          dp[i][j][t] = Math.max(dp[i][j][t], (i+1<=j-1? dp[i+1][j-1][t] : 0) + 2);
        }
        if (t > 0) {
          dp[i][j][t] = Math.max(dp[i][j][t], dp[i+1][j][t-1], dp[i][j-1][t-1]);
        }
      }
    }
  }
  return dp[0][n-1][k] || 0;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you optimize to O(nk) space while preserving O(n^2 k) time?
- How does the solution change if deletions must be exactly k?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests a constrained subsequence DP with gap bounds and reconstruction, a common real-world scheduling/selection pattern.

## Key Concepts

- DP on sequences with restricted gaps
- State dp[i][t] and transition over a window [i-U, i-L]
- Path reconstruction with parent pointers

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxSumWithGaps(A, k, L, U){
  const n = A.length, NEG = -1e15;
  const dp = Array.from({length: k+1}, ()=> Array(n).fill(NEG));
  const par = Array.from({length: k+1}, ()=> Array(n).fill(-1));
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++) dp[1][i] = A[i];
  for(let t=2;t<=k;t++){
    for(let i=0;i<n;i++){
      let best = NEG, bestj = -1;
      for(let j=i-U;j<=i-L;j++){
        if(j>=0 && dp[t-1][j] > best){ best = dp[t-1][j]; bestj = j; }
      }
      if(bestj!=-1){ dp[t][i] = A[i] + best; par[t][i] = bestj; }
    }
  }
  let end = -1, best = NEG;
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++) if(dp[k][i] > best){ best = dp[k][i]; end = i; }
  const indices = [];
  let cur = end;
  for(let t=k; t>=1; t--){ indices.push(cur); cur = par[t][cur]; }
  indices.reverse();
  return {best, indices};
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question introduces a new DP angle: partitioning a string into k segments with a per-segment distinct-character score, a practical proxy for text analytics. It tests state formulation, precomputation, and reconstruction, with a baseline O(n^2k) DP suitable for beginner to intermediate levels.

## Key Concepts
- DP on partitioning problems
- Precomputing substring properties in O(n^2)
- Reconstructing cuts via parent pointers

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxScorePartition(s, k){
  const n = s.length;
  const dist = Array.from({length:n}, ()=> Array(n).fill(0));
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++){
    const seen = new Set();
    for(let j=i;j<n;j++){
      seen.add(s[j]);
      dist[i][j] = seen.size;
    }
  }
  const dp = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const par = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){
    for(let t=1;t<=Math.min(i,k);t++){
      for(let j=t-1;j<=i-1;j++){
        const val = dp[j][t-1] + dist[j][i-1];
        if(val > dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t] = val; par[i][t] = j; }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction could be added here
  return {score: dp[n][k], cutStartIndices: []};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you reduce memory to O(nk) and compute dist on the fly?
- How does the approach change if k can be 0 or n+? and if n is large?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests a fresh DP angle: interval-based value depends on distinct characters within each substring, plus precise reconstruction for k blocks.

## Key Concepts

- Non-overlapping interval DP
- Precomputing distinct counts for all substrings
- Reconstruction via back-pointers
- Time/space trade-offs and potential optimizations

## Code Example

```javascript
// Implementation sketch
function maxKSubstrings(s, k) {
  const n = s.length;
  const dist = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(n).fill(0));
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    const freq = new Array(26).fill(0);
    let setBits = 0;
    for (let j = i; j >= 0; j--) {
      const idx = s.charCodeAt(j) - 97;
      if (freq[idx]++ === 0) setBits++;
      dist[j][i] = setBits;
    }
  }

  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const prev = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  const start = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));

  dp[0][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    dp[i][0] = 0;
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      // Option 1: skip i
      if (dp[i-1][t] > dp[i][t]) {
        dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t];
        prev[i][t] = i-1;
        start[i][t] = start[i-1][t];
      }

      // Option 2: end t-th substring at i
      for (let s = 1; s <= i; s++) {
        const val = dp[s-1][t-1] + dist[s-1][i-1];
        if (val > dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = val;
          prev[i][t] = s-1;
          start[i][t] = s-1;
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // Reconstruct substrings
  const result = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  while (t > 0 && i > 0) {
    if (prev[i][t] === i-1) {
      i--;
    } else {
      result.unshift([start[i][t]+1, i]);
      i = prev[i][t];
      t--;
    }
  }

  return { maxValue: dp[n][k], substrings: result };
}
```

Time complexity: O(n²k) for DP transitions + O(n²) for preprocessing. Space: O(n² + nk). Optimizations include limiting the inner loop or using convex hull tricks for special cases.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests a fundamental DP pattern with an explicit gap constraint, a natural generalization of the classic non-adjacent selection.

## Key Concepts
- DP over index and remaining picks
- Handling fixed gap via i-g-1 transition
- Space- and time-complexity considerations
- Reconstruction of chosen indices

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxSumWithGap(A, k, g){
  const n = A.length;
  const NEG = -Infinity;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const prv = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){
    for(let t=0;t<=k;t++){
      // skip
      if(dp[i-1][t] > dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t]; prv[i][t] = -1; }
      // take i-1
      if(t>0 && i-g-1>=0 && dp[i-g-1][t-1] !== NEG){
        const val = dp[i-g-1][t-1] + A[i-1];
        if(val > dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t] = val; prv[i][t] = i-g-1; }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction omitted for brevity
  return {best: dp[n][k], indices: []};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How to reconstruct the exact indices efficiently?
- How would you adapt to allow at most k picks or variable gap?

